findings.body:
The loop in `extractSlotFromKeysResult()` runs `j <= keys_result->numkeys` instead of `j < numkeys`. On the last iteration it reads `keys_result->keys[numkeys]`, which is out of bounds. When the keys fit in the inline buffer, that slot is a zeroed `keysbuf` entry, so `pos == 0` and the function hashes `argv[0]` (the command name) against the key's slot. For a single-key command like `GET foo`, that comparison almost always mismatches, so the helper returns `CLUSTER_CROSSSLOT` and `preprocessCommand()` marks the command `CLIENT_READ_CROSS_SLOT`. When `numkeys` exceeds the inline buffer, the heap array is one entry short, so the read is out of bounds and the write `slot_buffer[numkeys] = this_slot` is an out-of-bounds heap write.

::diff{file="src/cluster.c" lines="1110-1121"}

::callout{type="security"}
Out-of-bounds heap write from a client-controlled command shape: an attacker-supplied command with many keys reaches the bad index. Fix the loop bound before merge.

For the expected behavior, see `GETKEYS_RESULT_INIT` in src/server.h, which zero-initializes `keysbuf`. The loop should iterate only over the `numkeys` valid entries.

