`extractSlotFromKeysResult()` allocates `slot_buffer` with `malloc`, writes one slot into it per key, and never reads it back. The only work the buffer does is occupy memory and cost a `malloc`/`free` pair per call. The helper runs on every keyed command in cluster mode, through `preprocessCommand()` and `getSlotFromCommand()`. The allocation is also unchecked: if `malloc` returns NULL, the write dereferences it. It also uses libc `malloc` rather than Redis' `zmalloc`, so the memory bypasses the server's memory accounting.

::diff{file="src/cluster.c" lines="1107-1121"}

The function only needs `first_slot` to detect mismatches, so the buffer can simply be removed.

Before this PR, `extractKeysAndSlot()` always wrote `*slot`, setting it to INVALID before the conditional. Now `*slot` is written only when `num_keys > 0`. The only current caller, `preprocessCommand()`, initializes `pcmd->slot` before the call, so nothing breaks today. But the output parameter's contract is now implicit: a future caller that reuses an uninitialized `slot` for zero-key commands will get a stale value.

::diff{file="src/db.c" lines="3201-3203"}

Either document that `*slot` is left untouched when there are no keys, or keep the explicit initialization.

The branch `else if (num_keys >= 0)` follows `if (num_keys < 0) { return; }`, so the condition is always true when reached. The test is redundant and makes the control flow harder to read. Change it to a plain `else`.

